I could not reproduce the reported outage. From Campus 2 host pc1 (10.5.0.1) I successfully reached multiple 172.16.30.x hosts on Campus 1 with 0% loss and low latency: pc1→172.16.30.12/13/14 all replied (rtt ≈2 ms). Reverse direction is also healthy: 172.16.30.12→10.5.0.1 returned 0% loss. A traceroute from pc1 to 172.16.30.12 shows a clean L3 path with steady hops via 10.5.0.254 (default gateway) → 55.5.1.1 → 192.168.144.1 → 172.25.1.1 → 172.30.1.1 → 10.2.2.2 → 172.16.30.12, with no timeouts. 

On the control plane, OSPF on the inter-campus routers is consistent: router17 has ECMP routes to 172.16.30.0/24 via 172.29.10.1 and 192.168.144.1; router7 forwards that prefix via 172.30.1.1; router16 and router8 also advertise/learn 172.16.30.0/24 properly. There is no sign of missing routes, route flaps, or asymmetric blackholing along this path at present.

Root cause: No network fault is present now; the 10.5.0.0/24 to 172.16.30.0/24 reachability is currently normal end-to-end.

Corrective fix: No changes were required. Connectivity has been validated bidirectionally and the routing domain is healthy. If the user’s original issue was application-specific rather than basic IP reachability, please provide the destination IP/port/protocol so we can check for any ACL or service-level filtering; otherwise, re-test from 10.5.0.1 and confirm resolution. We will continue to monitor for recurrence. 
